"""Agent guidance exposed by Studio tools/list; not an automated listening gate."""

MUSIC_REVIEW_RULES = """Music production rules:
Generate songs, whole-song candidates and new song sections with Music3 only. ACE-Step is for bounded edits of existing audio, not new composition or song extension. Current Music3 does not support reference-audio continuation; never silently switch models or call a new composition preservation of an accepted performance. For full-song requests generate a complete structure and review an internal excerpt; do not compose a closed short song and append unrelated tails.
Preserving an accepted vocal means retaining that fixed performance, not merely its voice style. Before rearranging, establish the actual melody, tempo/beat grid, chord-change timeline and phrase boundaries from the source; prompt BPM/key are not measurements. A conditioned redraw can change harmony, rhythm and phrasing inside its region. Equal duration, unchanged vocal bytes and successful mixing do not prove musical compatibility.
Before presenting a rearranged song candidate, verify vocal-note/chord compatibility, beat phase, chord-change timing, phrase cadences and edit joins, and actually audition the combined section. Record evidence and unresolved items separately from technical checks. Diagnostic mixes may support this review but are not delivery candidates. If constraints or listening evidence are unavailable, report the gap and stop dependent generation/delivery; do not keep drawing random accompaniments or claim to have listened. User rejection prevents reuse as an accepted baseline and automatic mastering/selection.
Use concise display names: work title, version number and necessary purpose only. Store parameters, lineage and lessons in metadata or concise records. Mark rejected versions rejected in the source Project and remove them from ordinary preview/results; retain immutable history without presenting failed work as a current candidate. Do not delete shared assets or historical bytes.
These are agent execution/review instructions, not server-side musical quality detection. Tool success and technical preflight readiness never constitute artistic approval."""
